WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.700
- Hello and welcome everyone

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.060
to today's Brilliant Minds,
Bold Questions virtual talk.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.180
Our series spotlights high impact research

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.640
from across the academy
from computer science,

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.000
population and public health
to economics and today,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.020
molecular microbiology.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.120
If you are not familiar with us,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.960
CIFAR is a global research organization

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.530
that convenes extraordinary minds

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.240
to address the most important questions

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.470
facing science and humanity.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.140
My name is Rachel Parker.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.290
I am Senior Director of Research at CIFAR,

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.088
and it is my distinct
pleasure to introduce you

00:00:33.088 --> 00:00:34.040
to our two speakers for today's talk

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.270
on how do we get to net zero.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.130
First, we have Curtis Berlinguette,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.430
who is Professor of Chemistry

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.350
and Chemical and Biological Engineering

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.470
at the University of British Columbia.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.090
Curtis leads an interdisciplinary
team seeking ways

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.450
to discover and scale disruptive
clean energy materials.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.620
His academic group advances
fundamental science

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.450
and a range of clean energy applications,

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.383
including CO2 utilization
and advanced nuclear fusion.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.610
Also joining us is Yogesh
Surendranath or Yogi,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.180
who is an Associate
Professor of Chemistry at the

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.630
Massachusetts Institute of Technology.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:14.150
Research in Yogi's group aims
to use renewable electricity

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.680
to rearrange chemical bonds

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.940
by controlling interfacial reactivity

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.810
at the molecular level.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.530
The chemistry of these
interfaces is at the heart

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.330
of nearly all contemporary challenges

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.540
in renewable energy
storage and utilization

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.980
in a wide variety of technologies.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.250
Addressing this challenge
is essential for enabling

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.650
a low carbon energy future.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.730
Today's session will last
approximately 30 minutes.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.430
I will start the session with a question

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:43.500
before inviting our speakers
to interview one another

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.220
for about 20 to 25 minutes.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.930
And after this, I'll open the
floor back up to questions

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.020
from participants online.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.100
If you, as an online audience
member, have a question,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.900
you can use the Q&amp;A function to submit it.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.080
We ask that you not use the chat function

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.800
to submit questions.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.730
Lastly, the session is being recorded

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.703
and will be available on
the CIFAR website next week.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.860
Welcome Curtis and Yogi.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.270
So to get us started,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.750
we know that CO2 and
other heat trapping gases

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.100
are the main drivers of global warming.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.470
To avoid the worst
consequences of global warming,

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.350
we'll need to reach net
zero in our carbon footprint

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.050
in less than 30 years.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.810
Obviously this is no simple task.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.930
What is missing from the
current research agenda

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.570
that can aim to address the carbon threat?

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.111
- Thanks, Rachel.

00:02:30.111 --> 00:02:32.183
And good morning, good
afternoon, everyone.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.440
So Yogi, so chemists worldwide

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.750
are working to design catalysts that

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.510
will speed up critical
chemical reactions needed

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.040
to convert renewable resources,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.830
such as biomass into fuels and chemicals.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.693
So Yogi, what is a catalyst
and what does it do?

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.563
- Thanks Curtis.

00:02:51.415 --> 00:02:53.600
So catalysis and catalysis
science is really

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.470
at the heart of many of
the challenges we face

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.870
in decarbonizing the global energy system

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.170
and what a catalyst really
is, is a material, a molecule,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.270
it can be many things, but it's
function is what's critical.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:11.090
It serves the role of neither
being produced nor consumed

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.090
in the the overarching chemical reaction,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.997
but it serves a function
essentially chaperoning

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:22.530
the reactance towards
products across a low energy,

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.563
high efficiency pathway.

00:03:25.761 --> 00:03:26.710
And in doing so, it allows us

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.420
to carry out chemical reactions

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.400
much faster than without a catalyst.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.730
And in the cases of
energy driven processes

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.180
with much lower energy
input than we would need,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.450
if we didn't have a catalyst

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.290
or if we didn't have the
most optimal catalyst.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.010
The other thing a catalyst really does,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:48.010
it's equally important, is it
defines what the products are.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.260
It sets whether you turn a
reactant into one product

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.880
or 50,000 products

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.180
and how you choose which product you get

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.010
is critical to how much
energy you put in the process,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.680
how useful and valuable that product is.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.940
And indeed, if you think
about the conversion of CO2

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.540
into fuels or chemicals,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.810
you'd want the optimal distribution

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:15.030
of those fuel molecules
or chemical molecules

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.580
for a given application,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:18.010
whether it be in the aviation sector

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.460
or in other areas of our economy.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.840
But you don't want molecules
that would be deleterious

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.620
to their endpoint use.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.090
And so catalysis plays this essential role

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.560
in guiding reactions
towards one desired product

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.450
over another,

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.540
thereby minimizing the costs
that would otherwise accrue

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.340
from needing to do energy
intensive separations

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.640
of the products afterwards

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:41.960
and in allowing us to access the materials

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.510
that we would like with the
least amount of energy input.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.700
So let me turn the floor
back over to Curtis actually.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.470
So, you know, I'm a
catalysis science person,

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.410
but one thing that we've
actually learned over the years

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:57.230
is that the discovery of new
materials, new catalysts,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.620
new technologies for a lot

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.390
of these grand de-carbonization challenges

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.520
can take quite a long time.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.540
And many times we don't
even know the principles

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.390
or we don't know the unknown unknowns

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:11.420
on that journey towards new technologies.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.700
Curtis, I would like to turn
the floor over to you and ask,

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.800
what is your research group doing really

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.723
to shorten that development timescale?

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:19.733
- Yeah.

00:05:19.733 --> 00:05:21.740
And shortening that development timescale

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.310
is critical for us in terms
of solving the CO2 problem,

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.070
because we don't have
a lot of time available

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.203
to come up with with big solutions.

00:05:31.799 --> 00:05:34.970
And so one of the things our
group is very motivated by

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.293
are recent advances in
robotics and automation,

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:43.110
big data and machine learning.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.780
And what we're doing is
we're taking all three

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.760
of these aspects and we're bringing them

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.820
into the research laboratory.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.650
So that way we can
actually make smart robots,

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.090
to make self-driving robots,

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.550
that actually will help us make decisions

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:01.730
on the experiments that
we're making in the lab.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:06.730
And our mission here is to be
able to accelerate the time

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.470
it takes to take a new
discovery in the laboratory,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.590
out in the marketplace,
by at least tenfold.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.230
And one of the challenges
that we have with hard tech,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.310
commercializing hard tech

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.840
and taking things out of the laboratory

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.320
and into the marketplace,

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:23.060
is that it tends to take over a decade,

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.650
often a couple of decades or more.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.240
And when we look into the future,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.810
we want to have some CO2
technologies that are out

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.610
in the market within the next decade,

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.450
not two decades from now.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:39.450
And so what we're trying to
do is be able to leverage all

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.750
of these recent advances in automation,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.090
to be able to help us do that,

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:47.900
to help the researchers in
the lab collect more data,

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.930
to collect higher fidelity data,

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.080
and to also to be able to use algorithms

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.250
to help process the data
that's actually being created

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.970
and assimilate that information

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.780
into discovering new
materials, making new devices

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.933
and advancing those devices
and putting them into practice.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.100
You know, when we look back
over the course of history,

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.140
if we look at fuel cells, for example,

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.070
it's taken three plus
decades for fuel cells

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.520
to start hitting the market,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.140
which they're really just
doing over the last few years.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.520
And we really think that
we can learn information

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.970
from that particular sector
and help us accelerate

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:28.443
the development of these
different technologies.

00:07:29.437 --> 00:07:32.310
So this is something that
very much motivates us.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.170
- Curtis, if I can follow
up on that actually,

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.730
in the domain of using automation,

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:41.258
some of the challenges in
deploying green technologies

00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:42.550
is the challenges, the
unforeseen challenges,

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.300
that occur when they get scaled up.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.973
Talk to me about how
automation, if you feel it can,

00:07:48.973 --> 00:07:52.050
can even accelerate and perhaps
foreshadow those challenges

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.420
that may occur upon the
long term deployment

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.420
or the scale up of certain technologies.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.273
- Yeah.

00:08:00.703 --> 00:08:05.350
So I think automation can
help at various stages

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.543
in the development process.

00:08:08.645 --> 00:08:11.490
In the lab it can help
us find new materials.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.720
We can then potentially use robotics

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.220
to take those new materials

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.510
and to implement them into devices.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:20.110
And that process, that
step of actually taking

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.410
a brand new material and
putting it into a device,

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:26.133
that is a slow step in the lab today.

00:08:27.219 --> 00:08:28.970
Testing those materials in that device

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.053
is a slow step for today.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.630
And so being able to bring in automation

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:37.290
and particularly new algorithms
that are coming online

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:40.264
to help us process data
that are coming out of that,

00:08:40.264 --> 00:08:44.400
we're really confident that
we can shorten the timelines

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.993
from getting, from the lab
again to a commercial product.

00:08:49.354 --> 00:08:52.430
One of the challenges that we
have as researchers though,

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:56.763
is that it's really difficult
to put automation into action.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:01.440
Typically, what happens
is you have to buy a robot

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.660
from a vendor, but most
of these robots that

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.970
are commercially available
can only do one specific task.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:09.850
And most of the research that takes place

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:11.400
in the university environment

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.740
requires a number of
different tasks, okay.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:17.010
So human have the incredible dexterity

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.720
and this ability to pivot on the fly,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.890
to do different experiments,

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:24.600
to get creative and shift and
so one of the core features

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.440
of our program is we're
actually really trying

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.070
to promote this concept
of flexible automation,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:32.730
making robots that can
change on the fly with you,

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.380
change with the experiment and
evolve with the experiments

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:39.210
as you're understanding the
experiments start to change.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.850
And that way you're not having to build

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.020
a brand new robot for every single step.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.650
'Cause if you have to
build a brand new robot

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:45.560
for every single step,

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:48.210
that's going to be slower than
the current process today.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.550
So it's definitely not a solved problem.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.110
And there's several of us in
around the world right now

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:57.660
that are really trying to
more rapidly bring robotics

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.580
into the laboratory so that
way we can use automation

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.060
and machine learning
to its fullest extent.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.010
So Yogi, I know, you know,
you work on something

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.630
that I'm super interested in

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:12.570
and that's really the chemistry
at the interface, right?

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:15.000
That's really where
all the action happens.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.750
And so can you explain to everyone here

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:21.130
and how something so
seemingly small in scale,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:24.490
really just those atoms that
exist right at the interface

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:27.480
has such a huge impact on the properties

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.000
of the devices that we're making
for really making renewable

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:33.210
and capturing renewable energy

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.170
and converting that renewable energy

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:36.873
into useful forms of energy.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.380
- Yeah. Yeah. Thanks Curtis.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:42.810
The, you know, I've been
captivated in that problem

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.480
of interfaces and interfacial
reactivity for a long time,

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.201
really the entirety of
my independent career.

00:10:49.201 --> 00:10:51.800
And I got drawn to that by sort of,

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.270
a kind of a key point really,

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.923
is that one way I like
to think about it is,

00:10:56.803 --> 00:10:58.150
that a lot of the
processes we want to drive

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:01.860
involve moving things against
thermodynamic gradients,

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.030
moving, essentially rolling balls uphill.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:05.610
We want to take energy poor molecules,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.550
turn them into energy rich molecules

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.760
and electricity is a powerful force

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.730
for actually driving
those reactions uphill.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:13.144
And you can ask,

00:11:13.144 --> 00:11:14.910
so where does electricity,

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.440
carried in sort of macroscopic
wires meet molecules

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:21.310
and their inner conversion reactions.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.820
It turns out where that
occurs is at interfaces.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.840
It occurs in the interfaces
of our cell phone batteries.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.340
It occurs in the interfaces of a fuel cell

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.380
or electrolyzer that's
making renewable hydrogen

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.600
or converting CO2 to a fuel.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.090
And the interesting thing
that's so fascinating actually,

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:40.261
is that despite the critical
role that these interfac

00:11:40.261 --> 00:11:41.094
and the reason that's so critical is

00:11:41.094 --> 00:11:44.170
because that's where the
electrons that come in

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.170
via charge separation in a circuit,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.480
meet the molecules that they're
going to do reactions on.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.760
And so the properties of that
interface are so critical

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:55.690
to defining how catalytic
transformations occur

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:59.240
and particularly energy
relevant transformations occur.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:00.890
What has gratified us

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:02.380
and really been
interesting to think about,

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:04.438
and this is why I think, you know,

00:12:04.438 --> 00:12:05.410
CIFAR's and their support
has been been central

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.330
to this really is that the problems

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.800
around how we think about interfaces,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.300
despite how important they are,

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.190
the basic understanding
of how they function,

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.820
how they rearrange, how they degrade,

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.490
how they operate under
realistic conditions

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:24.340
is still very much in its infancy.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:25.860
We're trying to drive a Ferrari

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:28.600
without knowing how the parts
are put together in a sense,

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.533
in most of the devices that we make.

00:12:31.792 --> 00:12:32.625
And so you can imagine

00:12:32.625 --> 00:12:34.060
that when your cell phone battery dies,

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.170
that becomes a more of a phenomenology

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:40.650
than something that we
can look up in a textbook

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:42.590
and be like, ah, that's the reason

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:45.360
that this cell phone battery
degraded after this amount

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.860
of cycle number, right?

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:50.060
We are still at a place
where we get those pieces

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.720
of information from the laboratory

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.630
and we're sort of left
scratching our heads,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:54.590
wondering why that is.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.820
And that's because at an atomistic level,

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.860
we don't have a robust understanding
of how interfaces work.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:04.850
They provide these exotic environments.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:08.210
One of the things that makes
me so amazed is, you know,

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:11.460
the interfaces they generate
separations of charge

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.460
that generates some of the
largest known electric fields

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:15.940
in chemistry.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.890
You get enormous electric
fields on the order of tens

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.550
of mega volts per centimeter,

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.770
over the length scale of just a few atoms.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.720
And this allows you to
literally rip bonds apart.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.630
And we've been doing this in electro,

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.190
we've been ripping some of
the strongest bonds apart,

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:32.140
the bonds between
aluminum and oxygen apart

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:36.030
to make the aluminum that
we put in all of our planes.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.180
And we still don't even for that process,

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:39.940
know how that works.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:41.930
And so a lot of what
my group really studies

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.870
is how does at an atomistic level,

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:47.430
how can you use these enormous
electric fields that happen

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:50.180
at an interface to drive reactions

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:54.880
against their native
propensity to go downhill,

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.360
along a free energy gradient?

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.960
So we want to take energy poor molecules,

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:00.708
turn them in energy rich molecules,

00:14:00.708 --> 00:14:01.843
and the way we use it,

00:14:01.843 --> 00:14:03.680
the way we do that is by
taking interfacial fields

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.020
to literally rearrange those bonds.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:07.900
And we try to unpack those mechanisms

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.610
and then use that to
then design new materials

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:11.653
in a systematic way.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:15.567
- Is the challenge you're faced with today

00:14:15.567 --> 00:14:17.990
is that we just don't
have the tools available

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:22.705
to really dial into that
particular region of study?

00:14:22.705 --> 00:14:23.538
- Yeah.

00:14:23.538 --> 00:14:25.650
So the issue with
interfaces is this a needle

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:27.170
in the haystack problem, right?

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.740
Because you have the
bulk of your electrode

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:30.720
or your material,

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.150
and you have the bulk of your
solution or your electrolyte.

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:40.150
And even though those
are 99.9% of the system,

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:43.560
it's that 0.01% right at the interface

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.841
that defines whether the
whole thing works or not.

00:14:46.841 --> 00:14:50.550
And so we really have a paucity of tools,

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.370
if you will, we don't have the microscopes

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:55.130
to hone in on what's
happening in the interface

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.970
without the sort of clutter
and background and fog

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.800
of all the stuff that's
happening in solution

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.290
or in the solid.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.873
And that's really a core of the challenge.

00:15:04.873 --> 00:15:06.610
And one of the ways we've
really addressed that challenge

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:10.480
is not necessarily by
making a better microscope,

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:14.030
but by making better
ways to implant something

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.540
very precisely at the interface

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:20.120
and asking from that how does
that lead to specific effects?

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:22.960
So, in a way, we don't
try to measure the needle

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:23.793
in the haystack,

00:15:23.793 --> 00:15:27.400
we try to implant a very
shiny needle in the haystack

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.080
that can serve as a beacon for us

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:32.173
to understand that
outside of all of the fog.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:33.973
- Very cool.

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:38.240
- So speaking of an
interfacial problem, you know,

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.910
one of the interfacial problems

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.820
that both of us have a lot of interest in

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:47.820
is converting CO2 into
valuable chemicals and fuels.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:49.630
So talk to me about that, Curtis.

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:52.590
What can we convert CO2 into?

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:55.260
What are the useful things
that we can make it into?

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.860
And how would we go about doing that?

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.890
- Right, just to calibrate everybody,

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:05.890
so Yogi and I are very much
interested in finding ways

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.510
that we can use clean,
renewable electricity

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:14.510
to drive these transformations
of CO2 into something useful.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:19.580
And ultimately what the
commercial device will look like

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.930
is like a water electrolyzer.

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:27.470
So a CO2 electrolyzer, think
of it just as a big box

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:30.330
where you've got CO2 going in one side

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.920
with water and electricity.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.420
And then on the other side,

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:37.070
you're going to have like a byproduct

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:39.340
and you're going to have
some chemical byproduct

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.020
containing that upgraded carbon,

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:45.450
so that CO2 has been upgraded
to some carbon species.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:50.450
And the beautiful thing
about CO2 electric chemistry

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:54.210
is that there are a wide range of products

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.190
that you can make within that electrolyzer

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:00.430
by applying really the same
amount of electrical energy.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:03.660
The choice of product that
comes out on the other end,

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.990
all comes back to the
identity of that catalyst

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.910
that you put into the electrolyzer.

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:12.090
And this is, gets back to
the first point that Yogi

00:17:12.090 --> 00:17:13.250
was making earlier today,

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:17.510
the catalysts play such a
critical role in dictating

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:20.970
how that CO2 gets converted
into something useful.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:23.920
So what are some of the products that

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.610
could potentially be coming
out on the other end?

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:30.190
One of the products that we
like to make is carbon monoxide.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:31.580
Okay, so no one wants to go to the store

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.070
to buy carbon monoxide, I get it.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:36.500
But you can take that carbon
monoxide in a chemical plant,

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:39.460
and it ends up being a
very useful building block.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:42.296
It's a very reactive
species, and you can convert,

00:17:42.296 --> 00:17:43.700
you can polymerize that carbon monoxide

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:48.690
into useful plastics or other
carbon-containing materials.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.440
You can actually mix it with hydrogen,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.030
which is actually a
byproduct that comes out of

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:52.863
the electrolyzer.

00:17:52.863 --> 00:17:54.810
So you're effectively making syn-gas.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.770
And so there's an opportunity there

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.950
to actually just use that
product that's coming out of

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:02.660
the other end, and actually
do further processing

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.923
to convert it into fuels
that can then be used.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.940
There's a range of other
products that you make, you know,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.520
acidic acid, formic acid, ethylene.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.880
And there are now thousands of
researchers around the world

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:20.880
that are all trying to really
manipulate the catalyst

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:22.720
and the engineering of the electrolyzer

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.713
to be able to make these
different products.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.430
But one of the things that
we've been really thinking a lot

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.390
about in our group is just like,

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:34.620
if you could actually make
all of those carbon products

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.210
that, like all the plastics in the world,

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.700
everything outside of liquid fuels,

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:43.350
what, you know, how much
CO2 are you actually going

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:44.713
to be fixing?

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:48.800
And so this is actually
forced us down this path

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:50.450
of really thinking through

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:54.573
what type of product should
we be converting CO2 into?

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.170
And this exercise has
actually helped us converge

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:02.320
on really focusing on building materials.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:06.290
We really are incented to
try to figure out how can we

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:07.930
take CO2 from the atmosphere

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:10.420
and convert it into cement, for example.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:13.000
And why we like cement
is that if you're going

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:16.130
to take that CO2, convert it into cement,

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.580
that cement is going to be fixed in place

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.230
for several decades.

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:22.230
And that's one of the
other things that we have

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:24.300
to take into consideration in the choice

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:26.410
of the CO2 products that we're making,

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.180
because if we're just going to utilize

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:31.440
that carbon based product and combust it

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:33.820
and return that CO2 back to the atmosphere

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.370
in a very short timeframe,

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.630
we're not necessarily solving the problem.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:40.380
And so this is just another
challenge that we have

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:43.497
in the CO2 sciences is
figuring out what product

00:19:43.497 --> 00:19:46.480
are we going to make in which
we're fixing that carbon

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:48.400
for a really long time period.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.590
And so building materials seems like one

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:53.280
of those candidate materials
that not a lot of people

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:54.880
are looking at right now,

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:56.770
people are starting to look at that.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:19:59.020
But we see that as a huge opportunity

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:00.470
for our field moving forward.

00:20:01.544 --> 00:20:03.250
- Curtis, a corollary to that is is CO2

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.220
is only at about 400 PPM in the atmosphere

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:11.220
though rising fast, but that
doesn't seem very concentrated.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:15.930
How do we get the CO2 that
we would use to convert

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:19.450
into all of these valuable products?

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:20.620
- Right.

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:24.120
So we're really relying
on different technologies

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:27.610
to capture that CO2 either from air

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:32.610
or from right from the flue
stack of different plants.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.450
But ultimately we need
to solve this problem,

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:40.000
but really probably by taking that CO2

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.783
from the atmosphere in some way.

00:20:42.971 --> 00:20:44.571
And there are quite a different,

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.380
quite a few different
ways of doing that today

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.750
and lots of different
technologies that are out there.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.880
And I really think that what's important

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:57.620
for our community is to make sure that

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:59.860
our CO2 utilization community

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:03.120
is really thinking about
how that CO2 is captured.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:06.450
So that the challenge that
the CO2 capture folks have

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:09.790
is that if they have a technology
that captures CO2 readily,

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:12.260
that same material that captured the CO2

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:16.890
is not likely going to want to
give up that CO2 very easily.

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:20.040
And so, as a result, there's
an energy penalty associated

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.810
with releasing that CO2

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.850
so then you can either store it

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:27.180
or feed it into your CO2
electrolyzer down the road.

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:32.180
And so finding something that
captures that CO2 very quickly

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:33.560
and at scale,

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:36.010
and then is willing to release that CO2

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.560
with as little energy as possible

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:41.270
is really the grand challenge
for us in the CO2 sciences.

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:45.680
And something that our CIFAR
program is very motivated to do

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:48.730
is actually look at coupling
these two communities

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:52.020
of CO2 capture and CO2 utilization

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:53.570
and bring our different communities

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:55.410
and our different technologies together

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:58.480
to see if we can do
everything in one single unit

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:00.300
instead of operating as silos,

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:02.030
which really is how we've,

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:03.400
the two communities have been operating

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:04.600
over the last few years.

00:22:05.779 --> 00:22:07.740
So Yogi, I mentioned catalysts,

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:09.380
can you mention a little bit more about

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:11.750
what the specific properties of catalyst

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:13.416
that you're looking at

00:22:13.416 --> 00:22:15.966
and how specifically you
can tune that performance?

00:22:17.468 --> 00:22:18.301
- Yeah, sure.

00:22:18.301 --> 00:22:20.770
So catalyst design is a
kind of a grand challenge

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:22.910
that as I think Curtis
and I both talked about

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:25.440
pervades a lot of these
challenges in green technologies

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.023
and CO2 utilization.

00:22:29.107 --> 00:22:30.450
What we really look for in catalysis

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:32.940
is something that really
will accelerate the reaction

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:34.330
as much as possible.

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:38.330
That's sort of the rate at
which the catalyst turns over

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:39.950
the reacting into product.

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.510
Another feature we really
look for in a catalyst

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.190
is something that will
form a single product

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:46.410
over many products.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.940
That's the question of selectivity.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:50.840
The first one is a question of efficiency

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:53.100
or sort of performance of the catalyst.

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:56.060
Second one is related to the
selectivity of the catalyst.

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:58.960
And then there's a very key
and often overlooked parameter.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:02.040
That's equally important
in catalysis science,

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.820
which is the durability of your catalyst.

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.430
Whether its ability to
catalyze that reaction

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.580
and its ability to it selectively

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:12.700
to a single value added product,

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:15.040
does that remain the same over time

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:17.560
or after running it a
hundred or a thousand hours,

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.930
does it then degrade
and is no longer useful?

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:22.360
Of course you can imagine
that a catalyst needs

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:27.100
to have a durability on
the order of the lifetime

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:29.310
of the types of devices
we anticipate deploying

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:32.300
for CO2 utilization which we would imagine

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.280
are on the order of many decades, right?

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:36.710
So in the same way that the Curtis

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.390
and I have a shared interest
in looking at catalyst

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:41.560
for CO2 conversion while
the community has made,

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:44.900
I would say really amazing
progress over the last decade

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.430
or so in developing new catalysts,

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:49.720
new understanding of
their mechanisms of action

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:52.050
and new ways of controlling
their selectivity

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:53.430
and efficiency.

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.750
We still haven't yet gotten a good sense

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:56.840
for whether those catalysts

00:23:56.840 --> 00:24:00.160
will have the requisite
durability to be able to make it

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.943
in a device for two decades
or three decades, right.

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:07.200
And in many cases, it's
not easy to test that

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:08.373
in the laboratory.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:10.840
And oftentimes it's not easy

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:13.370
because in a real operating system,

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:16.690
your catalyst may be
exposed to tiny amounts

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:19.360
of foreign materials or poisons

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:22.230
or other species that you
don't often simulate for

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:23.320
in the laboratory.

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:26.810
And so that remains just a
very much an open challenge

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:27.930
for catalysis science,

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:31.683
but critical to the deployment
of these technologies.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.360
- So a follow up question on that,

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.483
if I could just interject here as well,

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:40.350
and share what the biggest barriers are

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.260
for the adoption of green technology

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:44.093
from a commercialization standpoint.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:50.470
- You know, Yogi just
touched on the big one

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:55.470
and that is proving out
technology can survive

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.430
in the real world for several years,

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.003
if not a decade or more.

00:25:01.466 --> 00:25:05.930
And we simply don't have
a lot of tools available

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:09.180
to us that allow us to do
the accelerated testing

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:10.660
that is reliable enough,

00:25:10.660 --> 00:25:13.110
that gives the people
that are going to go out

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.400
and commercialize these technologies,

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:16.550
the comfort to both invest in it

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:19.513
and to be able to introduce
those products into their plan.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:23.053
So durability, I would
say is probably the,

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:26.060
it is the one step that takes the longest

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:28.310
to prove out 'cause ultimately you have

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:30.580
to run the experiment
before a lot of people

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.840
will invest and use it.

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:36.700
And so coming up with
new ways to really try

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:39.230
to understand how we can get information

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:42.400
on how a system will behave
years into the future,

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:46.600
is it needs to become
a more important area

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:49.513
of science and engineering
for the community.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.190
I would say that the other limitation

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:57.150
until the last couple of years, of course,

00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:00.240
is really the capital
available to be able to go out

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:03.040
and pursue many of these technologies.

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:05.680
There was a lot of money that got injected

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.780
into clean tech in the
early part of the century,

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:13.300
and after 2008, a lot of
that capital disappeared

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:15.640
and clean tech became a very bad word

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.003
in the investment world.

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:20.030
What's happening today is now people

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:22.410
are a lot more sensitive
to global warming.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.600
They're impacted directly
by global warming

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.867
and now everybody's realizing
that we really do need

00:26:27.867 --> 00:26:28.700
to invest in this.

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:30.650
And so I'm actually really excited

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:34.570
to see how much capital is
coming back into looking

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:36.670
at clean energy technologies

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:39.250
and carbon capture, carbon utilization.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:43.100
These are some very hot areas
for investment right now,

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:45.320
and this is really how you drive change.

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:49.010
So I'm really excited to see the interest

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:50.240
and support that we're getting

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.160
from beyond the science community

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:54.917
that we actually weren't getting
even a couple of years ago.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:27:01.220
- That seems to me like a
perfect ending point for today.

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:04.390
Let's end on a note of
optimism for the future

00:27:04.390 --> 00:27:09.230
and hope that we've captured
people's imagination

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:10.840
and interest in the subject.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:13.110
And we wish you all the best.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:15.740
And thank you so much, Yogi and Curtis,

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.963
for taking your time
to talk with us today,

00:27:19.125 --> 00:27:20.690
we look forward to watching
your program in future

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:23.023
and thanks everybody for
watching it from home.

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:25.770
- Thank you.

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.917
- Thank you. Thank you to CIFAR.

00:27:27.917 --> 00:27:30.334
(calm music)

